  "answer": "To reduce the risk of constraint violations during reinforcement learning that could damage equipment or harm people, it has been proposed to fine-tune agents using the Constrained Policy Optimization (CPO) algorithm (https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10528). This mechanism works by utilizing a KL trust region and performance bounds that link KL-divergence to returns and constraints, which provides worst-case guarantees and limits constraint violations (https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10528). Specifically, the CPO algorithm linearises objectives, creates quadratic surrogate constraints, solves a small dual for λ and ν, and employs a line search to backtrack until surrogate constraints are satisfied at each iteration, thereby guaranteeing per-update constraint satisfaction (https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10528)."
